    <h1>RailVault Discussion Notes</h1>

    <div class="date">
        Notes from 2026.05.27
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Project Context</h2>

        <p>
            Internal brainstorming and criticism session regarding RailVault,
            decentralized infrastructure, railway preservation, adaptive reuse,
            governance structures, energy systems, and long-term sustainability.
        </p>

        <div class="quote">
            “Improve website with trolls.”
        </div>

        <div class="quote">
            “The future of RailVault is at stake.”
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>1. Arguments Against the Project</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                The project scope may be too large and complex to realistically complete,
                combining preservation, infrastructure, energy systems, governance,
                and digital archiving simultaneously.
            </li>

            <li>
                The target audience is unclear, potentially attempting to appeal to
                engineers, preservationists, municipalities, environmentalists,
                rail enthusiasts, and decentralization advocates at the same time.
            </li>

            <li>
                The project currently relies heavily on volunteer passion and idealism,
                creating concerns about long-term continuity, maintenance, and operational stability.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>2. Discussions</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Is decentralized infrastructure genuinely more resilient and democratic
                than centralized systems?
            </li>

            <li>
                Does industrial preservation protect culture and identity,
                or does it slow modernization and practical development?
            </li>

            <li>
                What is the value of digital archives if the physical infrastructure itself disappears?
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>3. Philosophical Questions</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Is RailVault driven by practical infrastructure needs,
                or by nostalgia for industrial civilization?
            </li>

            <li>
                Can decentralized communities realistically self-organize
                without eventually becoming centralized or fragmented?
            </li>

            <li>
                Are preservation projects attempts to resist impermanence
                and the disappearance of places, systems, and memory?
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>4. Engineering Questions</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Is converting railway coal carriages into battery systems
                technically and economically viable compared to purpose-built infrastructure?
            </li>

            <li>
                Can the system scale while remaining maintainable,
                safe, repairable, and compliant with regulations?
            </li>

            <li>
                Is adaptive reuse genuinely the optimal engineering solution,
                or mainly emotionally appealing because it preserves industrial artifacts?
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="quote">
            “Large to finish.”
        </div>

        <div class="quote">
            “Who is it for?”
        </div>

        <div class="quote">
            “Ambitious / overwhelming.”
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>5. Preservation Questions</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Does converting historical railway equipment into battery systems
                remove its historical authenticity?
            </li>

            <li>
                What infrastructure deserves preservation,
                and who should decide what is culturally important?
            </li>

            <li>
                Could RailVault itself eventually become a lost archive
                documenting infrastructure that no longer exists?
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>

